What appears to be not working correctly is the order of execution between
```C
        ATOMIC_ADD_FETCH(&lock->qp_group[qp_idx].users, (uint64_t)1,
                         __ATOMIC_ACQUIRE);

        /* if the idx hasn't changed, we're good, else try again */
        if (qp_idx == ATOMIC_LOAD_N(uint32_t, &lock->reader_idx,
                                    __ATOMIC_RELAXED))
```
and
```
    ATOMIC_STORE_N(uint32_t, &lock->reader_idx, lock->current_alloc_idx,
                   __ATOMIC_RELAXED);

    /* wake up any waiters */
    pthread_cond_signal(&lock->alloc_signal);
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock->alloc_lock);
```
so the assumption here was, that pthread_mutex_unlock has to do an atomic_thread_fence(release) for us.
But if David is right, the release on alloc_lock might be a slightly different thing than an
atomic_store_explicit(&lock->qp_group[qp_idx].users, x, release)
I have a version that does exactly this in #26964 but it needs also very intensive testing before we can
decide what to do next.
